 ],
 "summary": "Every lighthouse flashes its own pattern, such as two flashes every ten seconds, and that light characteristic is printed on nautical charts so a navigator can tell which light is which. From 1823, Fresnel lenses bent a lamp's light into a beam seen more than twenty miles out, and keeper-wound clockwork turned the lens so each sweep of the beam reached a ship as one flash.",
